# The New Nayanar

Tamil title: புதிய நாயனார்

## A Terrible Motor Accident

A speeding motor bus suddenly struck a tree and met with a terrible accident. Although there was no loss of life, many passengers suffered grievous injuries.

- Newspaper report

Alas, what a calamity! The wedding was to be tomorrow morning, and tonight there are blows and blood! The bride-to-be and the wedding party travelling with her have been injured and placed in danger.

- A passer-by

This is why I tell people to consult the day and choose an auspicious hour before undertaking anything!

- Astrologer Venkidu Sastri

Among the days ordained by God, what is bad and what is good, my boy?

- An elder

I undertook this only as an act pleasing to God. But some ill omen must have intervened. Perhaps this child cried, or that boy threw a tantrum. Who knows? Whoever opposes my service to Siva will not be spared, whether son or daughter!

- Kannappar

I told him then and there. Did he listen? Today he suffers. I have won.

- Manivasagam

My son Thanikasalam's horoscope says that the bride destined for him belongs to the household of an elderly Saivite whom the whole world will acclaim as a new Nayanar.

- Sivanesar

Tie the marriage pendant on the appointed day without fail. That alone is proper.

- The bridegroom's family

Appa, how can I enter a marriage that Siva himself dislikes?

- Thanikasalam

I escaped with my life! The woman who was about to die escaped because of the motor accident!

- Maruthappan

With the aid of these scraps of news, the history of Varikkazhuthai Nayanar has been composed. He is the sixty-fourth Nayanar, a new religious preceptor. The sixty-fifth will appear in a later divine play. Read on.

* * *

The bus conductor, Paramanantham, shouted, "Tickets, sirs! Those who have not paid, pay and get your tickets!"

Without even turning, the men continued their endless talk about one thing and another.

"Finish the business for which you came and tell your personal stories afterwards!" he snapped. Even then they remained deaf. They attached not the slightest value to his words.

"Young man, believe it if you wish; if not, let it go. I have firm faith in omens, scriptures and astrology. My elder boy must be about your age. His only occupation is contradicting me! He says his work is to abolish everything our elders established. He is my own son, of course, but such impudence must be beaten down before it raises its head.

"He has grown as big as a sturdy donkey, but has no sound sense!"

Thus the elder conducted his discourse. The other man, listening unwillingly, merely nodded and grunted, "Hmm."

"Young man, my daughter's wedding is tomorrow morning. Children nowadays speak contemptuously of consulting the day and stars. Today they received a fitting lesson. Have you ever known even an anonymous bus service to arrive at the stated time, even in a dream? The renowned astrologer Venkidu Sastri of Vengayapatti chose the hour. He specified a quarter past four this evening for fetching the bride. And the vehicle came exactly as foretold!"

Swelling with delight, he lengthened his sermon and added still more flavour.

"It is true that my daughter does not like the young man who is to become my son-in-law. And my son, Manivasagam, who has joined this argumentative party, left the house because of it. My daughter Mugil and Maruthappan love one another. He is a boy of our own caste, but their horoscopes do not agree. That is why I arranged her marriage to Thanikasalam, son of Sivanesar of Sangampatti.

"There is something remarkable in this, young man. I have long been possessed by devotion to Siva. I have served like a dog in the hope of becoming a Nayanar. Fittingly, that young fellow's horoscope says that Sivanesar's future daughter-in-law will come from the household of a modern Nayanar. See the divine purpose! I too shall become a Nayanar, and my daughter will marry into a suitable family."

He went on reciting the epic of his daughter's marriage without pause. Gopalu, who had listened patiently until then, grew angrier and angrier.

"Enough, old man! A fellow comes here with his stomach burning, and amid this misery you are taking my life as well. He says one must consult the day! Yes, the woman in my house said the same. Thinking I must not defy her, I consulted a good day and hour, and the god of justice delivered his verdict! For you it is an auspicious day to fetch your daughter. For me it is the death day of my case, the crooked hour when murder was done. You will become a Nayanar, and your daughter will tie herself to some monkey or other. Be quiet, sir!" he shouted, giving his agitation full liberty.

Everyone in that region knew Gopalu. He also bore the nickname "Lawsuit Gopalu." The court judge was his god. After selling house, cattle, land and forest, he had now lost that entire sum in litigation and become a picture of misery.

It was to him that the elder had been speaking, disregarding even the conductor's harsh voice. When Gopalu suddenly roared, the elder subsided.

* * *

Even the bravest drivers struggled on that road, but Mr Anonymous had made it his exclusive domain. Two buses plied the route, with no fixed arrival times. The road itself was full of pits and mounds.

Driver Durai was King Charles there. What he said was law. Passengers on that route took care not to displease him; otherwise he would condemn them to stand on one leg for an entire day. Tickets were issued if the crew pleased. If not, danger remained danger even if Siva himself came aboard.

Raise your voice slightly and ask, "Give me a ticket," and calamity arrived. You had to stand all day beneath the scorching sun. There was no one to question them.

No passenger understood why the anonymous bus service's last vehicle was travelling so fast that evening. Only Driver Durai knew. His face was drawn, making it clear that some incomprehensible trouble had occurred. He kept increasing the speed.

The motor ran fast, then faster still.

* * *

Kannappar was more than seventy years old, though he did not look so aged. He was a ripe fruit of Saivism. Sacred ash made a white spectacle of his entire body, applied in lines from head to foot. He desired to become the sixty-fourth Nayanar. Since Siva had already performed sixty-four divine sports at Madurai, Kannappar had long wished that the Lord would perform a sixty-fifth especially for him.

The young men of his village called him all manner of names. Naturally this angered him, particularly because his son Manivasagam led that "worthless crowd." Many secretly called him "Varikkazhuthai Nayanar" - the Striped-Donkey Nayanar. I whisper it only where it cannot reach his ears.

Had they called him a striped horse, he might not have minded. He would have read once of Siva turning foxes into horses, sighed twice and let the matter rest. But the word "donkey" doubled his distress. Dog, pig, eagle, peacock, bandicoot - call him any of those and he could somehow connect the animal with God and escape. At "donkey," his face shrank.

"That is the vehicle of Mudevi, sir, the mount belonging to Lakshmi's elder sister!" the mischievous boys would say, stirring his anger further.

He had a son named Manivasagam, who at a very young age had earned the bad name of being the village rationalist. His daughter wished to marry Maruthappan, but her father consulted the horoscopes and chose Thanikasalam.

Manivasagam pleaded that Mugil should be given to Maruthappan. His father refused. The son left home. His sister clung to her brother and wept. Her father rebuked and threatened her, unwilling to act according to her heart. With no one to console her, Mugil always lay sobbing. Her mother had already gone on her "pilgrimage to Sivaloka"; she therefore had no companion but herself.

They regard a woman as heated iron, imagining they may beat and shape her however they please. That was what Kannappar - yes, Varikkazhuthai Nayanar - believed.

It was he who had been reciting the marriage epic so engagingly to Lawsuit Gopalu: the canto of seeking a bridegroom, the canto of the son's departure, the canto of Maruthappan's love, the canto of Mugil's love for Maruthappan, and the canto of Mugil's lament in the raga Mukhari. He spoke without caring whether those opposite him listened.

When he moved on to astrological research, divination, the virtuous custom of fixing an auspicious day, and other such "noble matters," Gopalu erupted and poured out his anger.

* * *

Was the motor alone racing? The hearts of every passenger inside it raced too.

The moment the motor left the village, Kannappar's heart swelled with delight. He had feared some obstacle, but the vehicle arrived precisely according to the priest's directions, and so he danced like a pet parrot.

He was no impoverished Kuchela. He possessed enough property for the village to call him wealthy. Yet, dissatisfied with it, he tried to "sell" his daughter and become a Nayanar. What shame and sorrow!

Fearing the expense of hiring a private car, he had chosen the regular motor bus. That was why he narrated all his heroic exploits to everyone nearby, ignoring the conductor and continuing his story.

"Young man, did you hear this? My boy Mani said choosing an auspicious day was wrong. He went one step further and said bringing a priest to a wedding was an insult to self-respect. One of the worthless fellows with him even said -"

"Old man, please do not increase my heartburn! I am a man ruined after once living well. At the sacred hour suited to fetching your daughter, I lost my lawsuit. How can I accept it as an auspicious time?" Gopalu now spoke somewhat calmly.

"Everything happens according to each person's fate. Who can erase and rewrite what was written that day? Do not despair, young man. Think of him and pray every day. Our Lord, the beloved of Sivakami, will do you good."

Varikkazhuthai Nayanar had become a preacher.

"Where was this wisdom a little while ago?" Gopalu asked.

"You speak in anger, young man. Stop and think calmly," the Nayanar replied.

"Then why did you come searching for a motor bus? You could have kept your daughter at home and waited to see what God would do!"

The Nayanar was silenced. He turned. Tears stood in his daughter Mugil's eyes, and her friend was whispering something while wiping them away.

* * *

The motor continued, but Mugil wandered through a strange world from which she could not escape. Her mind competed with the spinning wheels.

It had been a night like this then too, with dim moonlight. The courtyard where she and Maruthappan had sat together would have looked just like this. Her father, arriving there like a sinner, had found the pair together. Even that did not make her cry as much as his determination to force Maruthappan to accompany the wedding party and witness this accursed spectacle until his heart broke.

One day, as Mugil rested on Maruthappan's shoulder, he had asked pitifully, "Mugil, today we are so happy. But I am poor, merely a servant in your house, a clerk who writes accounts for wages. What if your father refuses to give you to me?"

Tears gathered in his eyes and her throat tightened.

"Nothing like that will happen, my dear," she had comforted him.

She remembered it now, sobbed and muttered incoherently.

There seemed to be another pain lodged in her heart. Who could know what it was? Only she knew, and she continued weeping without saying anything.

She looked once at Maruthappan. He too gazed at her longingly. That was all; then came more tears. A young woman wept at the thought of marriage. A bud grieved at the thought of flowering.

The old man had piled phrase upon phrase about a golden, auspicious hour. Did he ever ask why this young woman should lament during that golden time? Why did she suffer while concealing some dreadful truth that gnawed at her heart? Was this an auspicious hour, or an hour for tears?

Days, planets, stars, omens and scriptures: all are stones placed in the path of human effort. Unable to understand this, lovers of antiquity bow before priestly arrangements. The Nayanar was one of the most deeply dyed in that vat.

Mugil thought of it all and wept. While her father went to choose a day for the marriage, she had chosen a day to elope with Maruthappan. But she herself abandoned that plan.

The very same hour brought joy to the Nayanar and tears and anger to his daughter Mugil.

* * *

Conductor Paramanantham sat beside Maruthappan.

"Which town are you going to?" he asked.

"Yamapattinam," Maruthappan replied softly, naming the city of death.

"Why such weariness? Have you grown disgusted with life?"

Maruthappan answered the conductor in a word or two and stopped. He began recalling all that had happened.

Mugil looked intently at him once, and his anguish began to flow like a spring.

The Nayanar boasted that the motor had arrived because it was an auspicious hour. Was this auspicious? They were tearing away Maruthappan's very life and giving it to a useless fellow.

They had checked compatibility, indeed! Who knew his misery? Had even Mugil's murmuring reached their ears? His friend Manivasagam had pleaded, but this old man's stubbornness would not move at all. He insisted on fulfilling his plan, even if a woman's life had to be destroyed.

Maruthappan was poor, but he had a heart. Within it lived the tender feeling of love for a woman, and the determination to win that woman and build a life with her. He made plan after plan, but Mugil was afraid. To elope, he lacked courage; to resist openly, he lacked money. Yet he wanted Mugil.

From the day he first touched her, he had lived believing that Mugil was born for him and he for her. Any careless words of his had been merely playful.

Now his castle of hopes had collapsed into dust. He was travelling with his beloved's wedding party to work at her marriage and watch another man tie the marriage pendant around her neck. What state could his heart be in?

The motor raced. His thoughts circled and returned always to one place. His heart kept singing, "Mugil, Mugil."

How can Maruthappan's mind be pictured? Words fail.

By consulting days, stars, scriptures and horoscope compatibility, they had cut apart a loving pair.

Maruthappan sat consumed by grief. He could not bring himself to come with the wedding party, yet he could not remain behind either. He was trapped beneath the wheel of distress.

* * *

Driver Durai kept increasing the speed. Why? During the Nayanar's discourse on the marriage, the old man had loudly described a quarter past four that afternoon as a golden hour. Whenever Durai remembered that same quarter past four, his heart trembled. He could not bear to think of the present hour.

His wife Maragatham had then been writhing in labour after three days of unrelenting pain. The midwife had tried everything, but the child had not been born. Lady Doctor Nirmala arrived and said instruments would be needed. She used them and delivered twins clasped together, their bodies wounded by the instruments. It was Maragatham's first confinement, and she was staying at her mother's house.

News reached Durai just as the motor was due to depart. He decided to complete this trip himself before going to her. That was why the vehicle's speed rose continually.

How was Maragatham now? Her condition must be pitiful. Might she have developed convulsions? People said such things occurred when delivery was difficult. Durai's mind became a cauldron of boiling oil.

He had been married to his beloved wife for barely a year. He wondered whether she might die. Tears filled his eyes.

* * *

The motor reached its greatest speed.

Every human being is a cave of experience. Each undergoes certain experiences and remembers them again. Some events may be sweet; other scenes may be bitter. So it was with every passenger travelling that night, each seated within a private world of thought.

It was about eight. Darkness surrounded them, and the road ran through dense forest with many bends. However fast Durai drove, he remained alert.

The motor twisted and wound along the road.

At that moment the mendicant's song, "Who knows what tomorrow will bring, O Supreme One?" held real value. Who knew or prevented the danger that would occur two minutes later? No one.

The vehicle took a bend at tremendous speed.

There was a sudden screech. That was all.

Ayyo! The motor had struck a tree. The vehicle was shattered, and many passengers were badly hurt.

Kannappar suffered a head injury and lost consciousness. More than ten members of the wedding party were bleeding. The bride, Mugil, was not badly hurt, for Maruthappan had lifted her bodily and leapt out before the vehicle was crushed. Even so, both sustained wounds.

* * *

## Terrible Accident

At eight o'clock last night, on the route operated by the Anonymous Bus Service, a motor vehicle struck a tree and met with a serious accident. The most poignant detail is that a bride due to be married the following day and other members of her wedding party sustained bleeding injuries and were taken to hospital.

A friend read this report aloud to Manivasagam. He laughed.

"I am telling you the motor overturned and that your father and sister are in danger. Why do you laugh?" the friend demanded.

"Yes, and you too must laugh with me. We ought to celebrate. Had this motor not overturned, my sister's life would have overturned instead!" Manivasagam replied.

"Your father lies on his deathbed, and still you make jokes!" his friend protested.

Manivasagam spoke louder and louder, unable to suppress his laughter.

"Why?" the friend asked, bewildered.

"Listen, my friend, and I shall explain. The news you just read is what the newspapers know. They do not know the truths woven before and after it, nor need they.

"I shall read something to you, but it is no article written for a newspaper. It is the history of Varikkazhuthai Nayanar. Do not laugh; listen.

"Because this motor accident occurred, my father became a Nayanar. My sister married the bridegroom of her choice. Maruthappan became her husband. And Manivasagam accomplished one part of his ideal.

"Thanikasalam took the accident as a sign of God's displeasure and declared that he did not want the marriage.

"Only now does the interesting part of the story begin.

"My father was a man destined to die; my sister was born to live. Yet his stubbornness sought to turn her life into a desert. Before I could execute my own plan, the motor accident lent me a hand and saved me.

"You are my friend, so I will conceal nothing. I had deliberately planned to create a disturbance in the wedding house and was following the motor in a private car. Otherwise, could I have administered first aid within minutes of the accident?

"You would be frightened to hear my plan. They say one must tell a thousand lies to bring about one marriage. I had planned to light that lamp with a lie.

"My sister had become pregnant. I intended to say that Maruthappan was the father. But after the motor accident I reconsidered.

"I wanted a plan that would accomplish the marriage without wounding my father's feelings or thwarting my sister's heart. I devised one and succeeded.

"I went to my father as he lay in bed. He looked at me eagerly, and I returned the look.

"'Appa, you are going to die,' I said at once.

"His face contracted.

"'If you have any unfulfilled desire, tell me. I shall carry it out,' I said.

"'Will you?' he asked, summoning his strength.

"'Certainly.'

"'I must become a Nayanar!' he said, all his longing shining forth.

"'Pooh, is that all? Appa, I shall certainly do it. The entire town already says you are a Nayanar.'

"'Do you mock me on my deathbed?' my father cried.

"'Do not be hasty. Wait and see,' I said, and left.

"I thought for a long time and finally reached a decision.

"Do you see this bundle of papers? It is the history of Varikkazhuthai Nayanar. It must be printed after my father's death. The most important episode, to be read with abundant laughter, is the Nayanar's attainment of salvation. My father has not yet died, but I have already written that part.

"Siva took the form of a donkey and appeared before the motor to claim the Nayanar. Durai swerved only to avoid running over him, and the vehicle struck the tree. Here, indeed, Siva became a donkey!

"By publishing this manuscript, I am supposedly to make my father a Nayanar.

"In return he had to consent to the marriage of Maruthappan and Mugil, and without a priest. He agreed. I conducted the wedding.

"Now he must become a Nayanar. Whether or not he becomes one, he has become a human being. By refusing to sacrifice a woman's life to ruinous orthodoxy, he has acquired the greatest merit of all."

Thus Manivasagam explained everything to his friend as they sat on the veranda.

"Anna, just look at Aththan! He keeps teasing me. He says I am the daughter of a Nayanar and that he is going to become the son of an Alvar. Aththan!" Mugil called as she came out of the room.

"What business has Mugil here?" Maruthappan said as he approached Manivasagam.

"Cough, cough..." From within, Kannappar continued wrestling with death.

4 June 1950
